\label{fact:rel-acc-bounds-distance-vi}
Let $G:\ensuremath{\mathcal{Z}}\mapsto\mathbb{R}^d$ be a $\mu$-strongly monotone operator and let $z^*$ be the equilibrium point. Then 
$\|z-z^*\|^2 \leq \frac{2\left\langle G(z),z-z^* \right\rangle}{\mu}$.

By strong monotonicity, for any $z\in\ensuremath{\mathcal{Z}}$,
\begin{align*}
    \frac{\mu}{2}\|z-z^*\|^2 \leq \left\langle G(z)-G(z^*),z-z^* \right\rangle \leq \left\langle G(z),z-z^* \right\rangle.
\end{align*}
The last step comes from the fact that $\left\langle -G(z^*),z-z^* \right\rangle = \left\langle G(z^*),z^*-z \right\rangle \leq 0$ since $z^*$ is the equilibrium.

